.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd September 25, 2026
.Dt BSDTRACE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm BSDTrace
.Nd administrator-authorized DTrace descriptor service
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the global provider for
.Dq system.Trace
version 1.0.0 from
.Pa /Capabilities/System/Trace.cap .
Each global connection creates a capability-mode worker.
.Nm
runs as root because opening and operating a DTrace consumer and submitting
its BSM policy records still require traditional kernel privilege.
The worker prohibits fork, IPC, incoming descriptor transfer, exec, and new
socket creation.
The supervisor admits at most 256 concurrent workers, retains their process
descriptors, and observes exit through
.Dv EVFILT_PROCDESC .
Managed shutdown stops admission, gives workers five seconds to exit after
.Dv SIGTERM ,
then terminates stragglers and reaps every worker before acknowledging
quiesce to
.Xr switchboard 8 .
Clients named explicitly in the allow list
.Pa bsdtrace.allow
may request one independently opened DTrace consumer descriptor.
All other clients are denied.
Wildcard policy entries are rejected.
.Pp
.Nm
is born in capability mode and never opens the allow list by path.
It calls
.Fn service_config_open "bsdtrace.allow"
from
.Xr libservice 3 ,
which resolves the name with
.Xr openat 2
relative to the read-only
.Pa Config
directory descriptor that
.Xr switchboard 8
delivers in the
.Ev CAPABILITY_CONFIG_FD
environment variable when the unit's bundle directory contains a
.Pa Config
subdirectory.
For the base-system unit that directory is
.Pa /Capabilities/System/Trace.cap/Units/bsdtrace.unit/Config ,
so the effective policy file is
.Pa /Capabilities/System/Trace.cap/Units/bsdtrace.unit/Config/bsdtrace.allow .
The historical path
.Pa /etc/bsdtrace.allow
is not consulted by the daemon; it remains only the default input of
.Xr tracectl 8
.Cm configtest .
.Pp
The allow list is opened without following symbolic links and must be a
regular file owned by the daemon's effective user with no group or world
write bits.
It must not exceed 64 KiB or contain embedded NUL bytes.
An absent
.Pa Config
directory or file is an empty, default-deny policy; an unsafe file is a fatal
configuration error.
The descriptor is close-on-fork, close-on-exec, transferable once, and limited
to the ioctl set required by the libdtrace consumer library
.Pq see Xr dtrace_fdopen 3 .
.Sh SECURITY
Raw DTrace authority is an administrator-only capability.
An ioctl allowlist cannot constrain D programs, probes, actions, targets, or
buffer allocation.
A successful client-side
.Fn tracecmp_dtrace_open
applies bounded RAM- and CPU-aware libdtrace buffer defaults, but raw
authorized clients can still change buffer sizes within the kernel's limits.
A provider-owned query and aggregation protocol is still required for
ordinary untrusted consumers.
Delegation and denial are audited and expose DTrace probes.
.Sh DTRACE PROBES
The
.Dq bsdtrace
provider exports
.Sy session-start ,
.Sy session-end ,
.Sy delegate ,
and
.Sy reject .
They report the authenticated client label, component instance or rejected
opcode, and a zero success result or
.Xr errno 2
failure value.
Idle pre-delegation sessions are closed after 30 seconds so an authorized
client cannot retain an unused DTrace consumer indefinitely.
No D program, DOF, or probe-selection payload is exposed.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "Config/bsdtrace.allow"
.It Pa Config/bsdtrace.allow
Explicit switchboard-authenticated labels allowed to receive raw DTrace
authority, one per line, opened through the delivered
.Pa Config
directory descriptor of the unit
.Pq Pa /Capabilities/System/Trace.cap/Units/bsdtrace.unit/Config/bsdtrace.allow .
The file must be root-owned and must not be group- or world-writable.
.It Pa /etc/bsdtrace.allow
Default file validated by
.Xr tracectl 8
.Cm configtest ;
not read by
.Nm .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtrace 1 ,
.Xr openat 2 ,
.Xr libservice 3 ,
.Xr libtracecmp 3 ,
.Xr capsicum 4 ,
.Xr switchboard 8 ,
.Xr tracectl 8
